// Intentionally no User model here.
//
// Identity is Neon Managed Better Auth: Neon runs the auth server and owns a `neon_auth`
// schema inside this same database (confirmed by introspecting it directly — it holds
// user/session/account/verification plus its organization-plugin tables, none of which we
// use). We never model or migrate any of it through Prisma:
//
//   - Prisma's multiSchema support would require declaring `neon_auth` on the datasource,
//     which makes every table in that schema part of what `prisma migrate dev` diffs against.
//     Neon's tables didn't come from our migration history, so every future `migrate dev` —
//     not just the first one — would report them as drift and offer to reset the database.
//   - We don't need a Prisma relation into it anyway: Better Auth's own session object already
//     carries the signed-in user's name/email, so our services get those from the session, not
//     a join. All our tables need from identity is the actor's `userId` (a UUID) to stamp onto
//     their own rows.
//
// So: every `userId`-shaped column in this schema is a plain `String @db.Uuid` scalar, with no
// `@relation`. The real foreign key to `neon_auth.user(id)` is added by hand-written SQL in
// references/integrity.sql, applied as its own migration — the same pattern already used here
// for the CHECK constraints and partial unique index Prisma can't express natively.
